\f1\fs22 Alexander Shakti, police \'93lieutenant\'94 and feted media celebrity, manufactured his famous smile for the enthralled audience. Despite falling ratings, disillusioned sponsors, and increasing pressure to fake evidence, the show must go on. Specifically, the final episode recording for the day must go on.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 \'93Good evening, my friends. Welcome to another episode of Candid Criminal,\'94 he said and paused.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93If you hadn't done it, we wouldn't have to vote!\'94 the studio audience chorused back, then erupted in applause and cheers.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yes, indeed,\'94 Alexander said. \'93Let's not keep our special guest waiting.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Guilty! Guilty! Guilty!\'94 the audience chanted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander chuckled and waved down the noise. \'93Airborne ladies and gentlemen, please join me in reviling Cloudborne Hamish Hammersmith.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The audience erupted as two giant bodybuilder types, their skin glistening with oil, dragged an emaciated sod before Alexander's raised dais and dumped him with rehearsed ceremony on the bare studio floorboards.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You have the wrong man,\'94 the sod said, as instructed by his production handler. \'93I haven't done anything.\'94 The audience laughed at his pathetic petition. Alexander hoped everyone streaming at home would laugh.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Play the package,\'94 Alexander said to camera, exactly as he had hundreds of times before. He hated the money the production team spent on the multiple holographic sequences of \'93evidence\'94 they played. But their design schtick was part of the brand: grainy, high-contrast, low colour saturation images, all calculated to cast the \'93special guest\'94 as loathsome and reprehensible. Each ended with a brand-conscious dissolve of Hammersmith's magnified, evil face.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Guilty! Guilty! Guilty!\'94 the audience resumed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>In the early days of the show, Alexander begrudgingly conceded the evidence packages were a major attractant of eyeballs, eclipsed only by his talent as celebrity anchor. Years on, he resented the production costs all the more.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The 
\fs18 QUIET PLEASE
\fs22  sign above the studio floor illuminated. The audience settled to murmurs.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What have you to say now, Hamish Hammersmith?\'94 Alexander read from the autocue. The next part of the show\'97where he challenged the special guest and tricked them into inconsistent testimony\'97passed with Alexander on autopilot. He\'92d given so many such performances, they were all a blur to him. While they were playing out, it wasn't uncommon for him to be reminiscing on a meal he'd once had, perhaps a spectacular mac and cheese.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It's time for the verdict!\'94 Alexander boomed, his engagement with proceedings reignited.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93If you hadn't done it, we wouldn't have to vote!\'94 The audience knew the formula.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Votes poured in. After voting time had expired, Alexander announced, \'93The votes are in, my friends.\'94 The audience hushed. \'93We received 2.17 million votes. There might be some folks voting more than once, but here on Candid Criminal\'85\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93\'85we value the enthusiasm!\'94 the audience answered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Oh, yes we do!\'94 Alexander sparkled at the camera. No one would deny he was a handsome son of a military conglomerate magnate mother (with all due fear and respect), but Eloise is for later in the story. Alexander gazed towards the back of the studio as if reading a faraway autocue. He beamed a smile, as if surprised by news. \'93The decision\'92s in, and it\'92s\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Unanimous!\'94 The audience drowned Alexander out, as they always did.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93There you have it, folks! Stay tuned for the sentencing. We'll be right back after these important messages.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Cut!\'94 the director called. Alexander went straight to makeup while an expectant carnival buzz pulsed around the studio.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>When recording resumed, Alexander parroted, \'93Thanks for those important messages from our sponsors.\'94 Turn to Camera 3. \'93Remember, for all your family planning needs, Pattinson's Mobile Abortions come to you and take your worries away. Just call 1800-2229-4663
\fs20 . 
\fs22 That's
\fs20  1800-BABY-GONE
\fs22 .\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Turn to Camera One. Alexander whipped up the audience with, \'93And now it's time for\'85!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The Wheel of Death!\'94 they responded. \'93Wheel\'85of\'85Death. Wheel\'85of\'85Death.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A large, intentionally low-rent chocolate wheel was wheeled out by a pretty extra in an elaborate headdress and swimsuit.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93How lucky can you get?\'94 Alexander said. \'93Each episode, we put ten randomly generated punishments on the Wheel of Death. Let\'92s see which will be Hamish Hammersmith's fate. Here are the options. Number 1: boiling in oil.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The audience went, \'93Ooh.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Number 2: trebuchet slap.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The audience went, \'93Ahhh.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Number 3 is clubbing to death. And we don't mean drugs and late-night dancing, folks.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The audience laughed less excessively than Alexander craved.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Number 4: shark tank. Number 5: one thousand paper cuts. Number 6 is acid injection. Number 7: packet of peanuts\'97the get-out-of-jail-free card, folks!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The audience booed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93But I'm anaphylactic,\'94 Hammersmith complained weakly.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The audience cheered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We know,\'94 Alexander said behind his hand to Hammersmith, loud enough so the mics would pick it up. \'93Number 8: snake pit. Number 9: drowning in a cesspool of fetid dairy products.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92m lactose intolerant,\'94 Hammersmith bemoaned.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93And finally, number 10: helium chamber. Your last words sound like a talking chipmunk, so choose them wisely.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93But I haven't done a thing.\'94 Hammersmith shook. His copious tears eroded the dirt from his cheeks and gave him an appearance of frothing at the mouth.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, c\'92mon,\'94 Alexander chided Hammersmith. \'93Spin the wheel.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Spin the wheel! Spin the wheel! Spin the wheel!\'94 the chorus ascended over the stomping of feet.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hammersmith, quaking, approached the wheel. \'93Oh, please! Someone help me!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>By perhaps the only grit of luck the wretched Cloudborne Hamish Hammersmith ever tasted, someone did help him\'97a squad of TRAMPS\'92 riot cops infiltrated the studio and escorted Alexander away.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Show\'92s over, folks,\'94 Alexander beamed as he was bundled out the exit.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 The Wheel of Death was rigged to stop at TREBUCHET SLAP, Alexander's favourite episode climax both for its effect on ratings and for the production intricacies it required to pull off. While all execution scenes were, by necessity, one-take wonders\'97there could be no do-overs\'97the trebuchet slap in particular required perfect timing and execution. 
\f2\i Candid Criminal\'92s
\f1\i0  trebuchet was the typical medieval siege engine: sturdy wooden A-frame set upon a wheeled platform complete with hulking iron brackets, giant round-head nails, ropes and pulleys. A thick beam about six metres long pivoted on an axle attached to the A-frame's apex. At one end of the beam hung a large counterweight; at the other, a lengthy sling and pouch where the convict would be strapped in. The counterweight would be winched up and ratcheted in place. When the executioner (a swimsuit model, as mandated by every focus group ever) released the ratchet, the counterweight would fall, and over would sling convict, cradle and all. The magic of 
\f2\i Candid Criminal's
\f1\i0  corrupted trebuchet was that it would not hurl its payload into the air, such as when laying siege to a castle. No, this trebuchet, by perversion of its design, hurled its hapless passenger straight into the concrete floor within splintered reach of its wheels.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 Convict pancake, anyone?\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Yes, the trebuchet slap was Alexander's favourite. It was difficult to perfect. In the early days, if a convict appeared to survive the engine, they were pardoned. But freeing convicts stuck awkwardly in the audience\'92s craw, and eventually Marketing demanded a survivor be subject to a second spin of the wheel, usually subsequently rigged to 
\fs18 CLUBBING
\fs22 . As much as his fans loved a double event, Alexander preferred a successful first execution. He was a professional.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Alexander complained all the way from the studio to the oversized TRAMPS hover-copter cruiser\'97half bayou fan-boat, half dragonfly, half bloated pig. \'93You couldn\'92t wait for me to finish?\'94 he demanded.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

