\f1\fs22 \'93We gotta get there before the ambos,\'94 a riot cop said.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Shit
\f1\i0 , Alexander thought. 
\f2\i Must be serious
\f1\i0 . \'93Get where?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Wheels up in ten seconds,\'94 was all the answer he got.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Stuffed in the back, Alexander seethed. The second time today he\'92d been kidnapped by his own department. Earlier, he\'92d copped the morning\'92s suicide case, his punishment for Mum's influence, he knew. He hated investigative work. He was indolent about it; celebrity duties were far more entertaining and less work-like. Most of his colleagues despised him, convinced his good fortune was purely down to his mother's curation. So unfair! They'd rather envy him than credit his actual talent.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He\'92d learned quickly he was assigned to such cases exactly because he was so lackadaisical. If word came down he was assigned to a suicide\'97possibly from as high up as the Commissioner\'97he knew to do the minimum, then slip away to a nightclub for recreational brain bleaching until the sun came up. As it turned out, the case was obviously suicide. Alexander found the weapon\'97a shotgun, would you believe?\'97with the deceased\'92s prints on it. Now, with Alexander\'92s prints all over the case file, it would remain forever 
\fs18 CLOSED
\fs22 . The whole department knew why he got these jobs: he was perfect for a cover-up. The odds of anyone reopening a file the son of Eloise Shakti closed were nil.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>What he hated most about those jobs: there was never a script. No\'97second worst. The worst was, he wasn\'92t allowed to take a makeup crew.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

